<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dynamis (Bosporan queen)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 8 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dynamis was queen of the Bosporan Kingdom in the late first century BCE, a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharnaces-ii-of-pontus">Pharnaces II</a> and granddaughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI of Pontus</a>. She married Asander and, after his death around 17 BCE, briefly the usurper Scribonius and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polemon-i-of-pontus">Polemon I of Pontus</a>, whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> supported. She later ruled in her own right with Roman approval and died around 8 CE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dynamis-bosporan-queen.jpg" alt="Dynamis (Bosporan queen)" width="320" height="466" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_a_bosporan_queen_in_the_Hermitage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pharnaces II, Asander and his wife <b>Dynamis</b>, Polemon I and then Aspurgus ruled with Roman approval, and during the first century CE the kings took the names Tiberius Julius, styled themselves friends of Caesar and of the Romans, and placed the emperor’s portrait on their coins.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bosporan-kingdom">Bosporan Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
